-- title:
--   Invariance of the height-normalised fine family over Noetherian bases
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are distinct primes ($hrr$), $N$ is a nonzero squarefree integer divisible by neither ($hrN$, $hrbarN$, $hN$).
--
--   **Base rings.** $\mathcal{O}$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), $\pi\in\mathcal{O}$ is irreducible (`hπ`), $\mathcal{O}$ is $\pi$-adically complete (`hcomplete`), $\#(\mathcal{O}/\pi)=r$ (`hres`) and $(r)=(\pi)$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal{O}$. Further, $Onr$ is a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, complete for the ideal $(\pi)$ (`hOnr_complete`), with $(\pi)$ maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $Onr$ satisfying a monic polynomial over $\mathcal{O}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree having a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}\pmod{\pi}$ (`hFr`); thus $Onr$ plays the role of the completion of the maximal unramified extension and $\mathrm{Fr}$ of its Frobenius. Finally $\mathrm{vdet}:\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb{Z}$ (written multiplicatively) is a homomorphism which, by `hvdet`, sends $g$ to $n$ exactly when $\det g=u\pi^{n}$ for some $u\in\mathcal{O}^{\times}$; it is the $\pi$-valuation of the determinant.
--
--   **Indefinite side and moduli.** $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ is indefinite and ramified exactly at $r$ and $\bar r$ (`hB`), $\Lambda$ is a maximal order in it (`hΛ`). The scheme $\mathcal{X}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$ with the point map $pt$ is a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure (`h𝒳`: isomorphism-invariance, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on geometric points of algebraically closed fields, and the universal property). An integer $n\ge 3$ is prime to $r$, $\bar r$ and $N$ (`hn`, `hrn`, `hrbarn`, `hnN`), and $(M,f_M,ptF)$ is a fine moduli space for such curves equipped with a full level-$n$ structure (`hM`). A group $G$ acts on $M$ by $\rho$ with labels $\chi:G\to\Lambda$, this action being a level-twist action in the sense of `hρ` (action over the base, twisting of level structures by $\chi$, and the labelling axioms modulo $n$). The morphism $p:M\to\mathcal{X}$ lies over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$ (`hp`), is invariant under $\rho$ (`hρp`) and forgets the level-$n$ structure on points (`hp_pt`). For each prime $\ell\neq r,\bar r$, $(\mathcal{Y}_\ell,g_\ell,ptT_\ell)$ is a coarse moduli space for curves with an extra level-$\ell$ subgroup (`h𝒴`), and $d_0,d_1:\mathcal{Y}_\ell\to\mathcal{X}$ are the two degeneracy maps over $\mathcal{O}$ (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`), $d_0$ forgetting the subgroup (`hd₀`) and $d_1$ passing to a level isogeny quotient (`hd₁`). Endomorphisms $a_r,a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal{X}$ over $\mathcal{O}$ (`harf`, `harbarf`) realise the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **Definite side and the group $\tilde\Gamma$.** $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a_1,b_1]$ is definite and ramified exactly at $\bar r$ (`hdef`); $\Lambda_1$ is maximal, $R_1\le\Lambda_1$ is an Eichler order of level $N$ (`hΛ₁`, `hR₁`, `hRΛ₁`), $n_1$ lies in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ (`hn₁`) and $R_1\cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1}$ is Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`); $\iota_0$ is an injective $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra map into $M_2(K_0)$ (`hι₀`) and $v$ is the height-one prime containing $r$ (`hv`). The subgroup $\Gamma_t$ consists of the units of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a_1,b_1]$ lying in the local unit boxes of $R_1$ at all places other than $v$ (`hΓt`). Families $s_\ell$, $sf_\ell$ indexed by the primes $\ell\neq r,\bar r$ satisfy `hs`: $sf_\ell$ has local component $s_\ell$ away from $r$ and is trivial at $r$, the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set of $(\Lambda_1,R_1\cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1})$ at $\ell$ when $\ell\mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of $R_1\cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1}$ at $\ell$ otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell)=\ell$; $\Gamma_{t,\ell}=\Gamma_t\cap s_\ell\Gamma_ts_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). The unit $\bar w$ has reduced norm $\bar r$ and normalises $\Gamma_t$ (`hwbar`). Integers lie in $\Lambda$ (`hΛℤ`), and $\mathrm{coord}:\Lambda\to W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})^2$ is an order coordinate system (`hcoord`: additive, unital, twisted-multiplicative, injective, dense and trace-compatible).
--
--   **Special fibre, formal modules and the action of $\Gamma_t$.** $A_0$ is a fake elliptic curve over $Onr/\pi$, $X_0$ a formal module over $Onr/\pi$ for the maximal order of the quaternion division algebra at $r$ (a two-dimensional commutative formal group with a $W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})$-action and $\varpi$ with $\varpi\circ\varpi=[r]$, $\varpi\circ[a]=[a^{\sigma}]\circ\varpi$) of height $4$ (`hX₀`), $\theta_0$ a system of formal coordinates for $A_0$ in two variables, and $A_0$ is a formal module via $\mathrm{coord}$, $X_0$, $\theta_0$ (`hA₀`). Maps $e,e':\Gamma_t\to\operatorname{End}(A_0)$ over the base (`he`) with degrees $\deg$ satisfy: $(e\gamma,e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level-$N$ structure (`hE1`), multiplicativity up to powers of $r$ (`hE1mul`), the value on rational scalars up to powers of $r$ (`hE1sc`), and conversely only scalars act by multiplication (`hE1'`); `hE2` asserts that for every injective ring map $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})$-action and $\varpi$ in $\operatorname{End}(X_0)$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$ is induced on formal coordinates by an $\varepsilon$ in that centraliser with $E_0\varepsilon=r^{k_\gamma}g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$; `hE3` provides labels $\Gamma_t\to\Lambda$ describing the action of $e\gamma$ on the chosen level-$n$ structure, multiplicative and scalar-valued modulo $n$; `hE4` asserts that over an algebraically closed field every level-preserving $r$-power isogeny of a pullback of $A_0$ comes from some $e\gamma$ up to powers of $r$. An order $R_2\le R_1$ with $r$-power denominators in $R_1$ (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`, `hR₂r`) acts through $\hat e$ over the base (`hê`), compatibly with the group law, the $\Lambda$-action and the level structure (`hE5a`), unitally and multiplicatively and on integers (`hE5b1`, `hE5b2`, `hE5b3`), with the Rosati relation for reduced norms (`hE5c`), compatibly with $e$ on $r$-power multiples of elements of $\Gamma_t$ (`hE5d`), and matching $\iota_0$ simultaneously for $\Gamma_t$ and $R_2$ through a common $g$ (`hE5e`).
--
--   **The Drinfeld side.** $\iota:W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})\to Onr$ is a ring map, $\Phi$ is a formal module over $Onr/r$ which is special for the induced structure map (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`); $MD$ is a moduli package over $Onr$ satisfying the Zariski sheaf condition (`hMD`), and $\eta$ assigns to rigidified objects of $\Phi$ points of $MD$; `hη` has three clauses: on Noetherian bases admissible rigidified objects have the same image under $\eta$ exactly when they are isomorphic, $\eta$ is natural in the base, and every point of $MD$ is locally represented by an admissible rigidified object. $E_0$ is an injective ring map from the centraliser of the action on $\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal{O})$ in the precise sense of `hE₀`. The family $eD$ compares, over Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebras in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, the functor of $MD$-points with the product of the functor of $Onr$-algebra maps and Drinfeld's functor $\Omega$ of Deligne data; it is natural (`hnatD`), bijective (`hbijD`), compatible with the structural $Onr$-algebra map (`hfstD`), matches the $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-action on $MD$-points with the twisted action on pairs (`hGLD`), sends $\pi$-translation to the Frobenius twist of the first component (`hPiD`), and $\pi$-translates exist (`hPiexD`). The map $\kappa:Onr/\pi\to Onr/r$ is compatible with the two quotient maps (`hκ`), and $\beta_0$ is an isogeny from $\Phi$ to the reduction of $X_0$ along $\kappa$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`). $P_0$ is a full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner datum at $\bar r$, the comparison functor $\Xi$, and the clauses $\mathrm{hK}$, $\mathrm{hC}$.** $A_{0w}$ together with $aw,aw',bw,bw'$ and $k_w$ realises the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ (`hALw`) followed by a level-preserving isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$ (`hBSw`, `hBSwlev`, with the compatibilities `haw`, `haw'`, `hbw`, `habw`), and `hJOINTw` matches $\Gamma_t$ and $\bar w$ simultaneously through $\iota_0$ on the formal module. The family $\Xi$ sends rigidified curves over a Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent to points of the $MD$-functor; `hΞleg` says that over connected bases its structural map is a Frobenius twist of the given one, `hΞnat` that it is natural for pullbacks, `hΞiso` that it is unchanged under isomorphisms of rigidified curves in the stated sense, and `hΞdef` computes it explicitly as $\eta$ applied to an admissible rigidified transport of $\theta_0$, $\kappa$, $\beta_0$ with twist $j\le 1$. For the fixed $g_0\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, `heqΓ` and `heqW` state that translating a rigidification by $e\gamma$, resp. passing to the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$, corresponds under $\Xi$ to the action of $r^{c}g_0\iota_0(\gamma)g_0^{-1}$, resp. $r^{c}g_0\iota_0(\bar w)g_0^{-1}$. The clauses `hK1`–`hK4` express the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison for curves normalised by $g_0$: existence of a rigidified curve matching a given Deligne datum over a connected base (`hK1`), uniqueness up to an isomorphism compatible with the rigidifications (`hK2`), compatibility with base change (`hK3`), and lifting of rigidifications along a surjection with square-zero kernel (`hK4`). The clauses `hC0e`, `hC0u` give existence and uniqueness of the normalised level-$n$ transport of $P_0$ along a rigidification.
--
--   **The family $\Theta_f$ and the level homomorphism.** $\Theta_f$ assigns, to each Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and each triple consisting of an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $Onr\to B$, a Deligne datum over $B$ and an element of $G$, a morphism $\operatorname{Spec}B\to M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$. It is natural in the base (`hnat`), satisfies $\Theta_f(x,gh)$ composed with $\rho(h)$ equals $\Theta_f(x,g)$ (`hG`), and at the identity of $G$ it is given by $ptF$ applied to the curve with its normalised level-$n$ structure (`hC2`) and, after composing with $p$, by $pt$ applied to the curve (`hC2c`). Finally $\theta_t:\Gamma_t\to G$ is a group homomorphism, `hC3` describes how a twisted translate of a Deligne datum by $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$ changes the rigidification, moving the level-$n$ structure by the label $\chi(\theta_t\gamma)$ and leaving the extra level-$\ell$ structures unchanged for primes $\ell\ne r$ invertible in the base, and `hC5` is the fibre clause over algebraically closed fields: two rigidified curves with the same underlying Deligne data are isomorphic exactly via some $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$, with the corresponding effect on level-$n$ and level-$\ell$ structures.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every commutative Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $C$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, all $x_1=((\psi_1,d_1),g_1)$ and $x_2=((\psi_2,d_2),g_2)$ consisting of $\mathcal{O}$-algebra maps $\psi_i:Onr\to C$, Deligne data $d_i$ over $C$ and elements $g_i\in G$, and every unit $\gamma$ of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a_1,b_1]$ lying in $\Gamma_t$, the following three conditions imply $\Theta_f(x_2)=\Theta_f(x_1)$ as morphisms $\operatorname{Spec}C\to M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$:
--
--   (i) $d_2$ is the pullback of $d_1$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, that is, for every full $\mathcal{O}$-lattice $M$ in $K_0^2$ the line of $d_2$ at $M$ is the preimage, under the base-changed isomorphism $C\otimes M\to C\otimes\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}M$, of the line of $d_1$ at $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}M$;
--
--   (ii) for every $y\in Onr$ with the property that $\mathrm{Fr}^{\,\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0(z))}y=y$ for all $z\in\Gamma_t$ that are rational scalars $c\cdot 1$ and satisfy $\theta_t(z)=1$, one has $\psi_2(y)=\psi_1\bigl(\mathrm{Fr}^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0(\gamma))}y\bigr)$;
--
--   (iii) $g_2=\theta_t(\gamma)\,g_1$ in $G$.
--
--   Conditions (i) and (ii) together are a weakening of the assertion that $(\psi_1,d_1)$ and $(\psi_2,d_2)$ are related by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$: the equality of the structural maps up to Frobenius twist is demanded only on the subring of elements invariant under all the Frobenius powers attached to central elements of $\Gamma_t$ in the kernel of $\theta_t$.
--
--   This is the invariance step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld $r$-adic uniformisation of the Shimura curve attached to an indefinite quaternion algebra ramified at $r$ and $\bar r$: it says that the height-normalised family of $\pi$-nilpotent points of the fine moduli space $M$ is blind to the diagonal action of $\Gamma_t$ on pairs consisting of a Deligne datum for the formal upper half-plane and a point of the level group $G$, the Frobenius condition on the unramified structural map being imposed only on the relevant invariants. It feeds the comparison of fibres over algebraically closed fields, where the resulting map is shown to identify points exactly up to the action of $\Gamma_t$.
